---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";

const DOCS = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs`;

const description =
  "Who and what can act on an Orca stack: UI logins and API tokens, worker tokens scoped to a pool, the per-machine boot token of an autoscaled pool, the per-run dista cluster token, pipeline secrets that specs name but never carry, SOPS on deploy, and where the Hetzner, AWS and registry credentials live.";

const tokens = [
  ["UI session", "orca_session cookie, 32 random bytes", "a user's browser", "ORCA_UI_SESSION_HOURS (12)", "logout, password change, user removed"],
  ["API token", "orca_ and 64 hex digits", "orca login on a laptop", "ORCA_API_TOKEN_DAYS (30)", "orca logout, orca user revoke"],
  ["Worker token", "orca_ and 64 hex digits", "one remote worker", "--days, at most ORCA_WORKER_TOKEN_DAYS (365)", "orca worker-token revoke"],
  ["Boot token", "a worker token", "one autoscaled machine", "30 days", "revoked when the machine is removed"],
  ["Cluster token", "DISTA_TOKEN", "one run start's dista processes", "that run start", "ends with the run start"],
];
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/security" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Operations</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Tokens and secrets</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every credential in Orca has one job and a short reach. Postgres holds only hashes of
    tokens and the names of secrets, a worker never holds database credentials, and a
    pipeline's spec names its secrets without carrying them.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
    <a href="#logins">UI logins</a>
    <a href="#worker-tokens">Worker tokens</a>
    <a href="#boot-tokens">Boot tokens</a>
    <a href="#cluster-token">Cluster tokens</a>
    <a href="#pipeline-secrets">Pipeline secrets</a>
    <a href="#deploy-secrets">Secrets on deploy</a>
    <a href="#cloud-credentials">Cloud credentials</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Credential</th><th>Form</th><th>Held by</th><th>Lasts</th><th>Ended by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tokens.map(([what, form, who, lasts, ended]) => (
          <tr><td>{what}</td><td><code>{form}</code></td><td>{who}</td><td class="ref">{lasts}</td><td>{ended}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The server keeps only the SHA-256 of a session, an API token or a worker token, so a
    copy of the database doesn't let anyone in. The <code>orca_</code> prefix lets a
    secret scanner recognise a token that leaked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="logins">UI logins</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who can reach the UI can read it. Its actions (start, backfill, resume,
    rerun, cancel, rebuild) need a logged-in user as soon as any user exists, or
    with <code>ORCA_UI_LOGIN=required</code> before one does, so a stack with users
    can't be left open by forgetting the variable.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`orca user add alice          # reads the password from standard input
orca user tokens alice       # her API tokens
orca user revoke alice 3     # or @all
orca user remove alice       # and ends her sessions`} />
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Passwords</strong> are bcrypt hashes made by Postgres's pgcrypto, so neither
      Orca nor its logs see one after it is checked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sessions</strong> are an <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Strict</code>
      cookie, <code>Secure</code> with <code>ORCA_UI_SECURE_COOKIES=1</code> behind HTTPS.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Failed logins</strong> are audited and counted: after 5 for one name, or 20
      from one address, in 15 minutes, <code>/login</code> and <code>/api/login</code>
      answer 429 without checking the password. Behind your own proxy,
      <code>ORCA_UI_TRUST_PROXY=1</code> takes the address from
      <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>API tokens.</strong> <code>orca login &lt;url&gt;</code> gets a token for the
      <code>orca</code> command on another machine, kept there mode 600, and sent as
      <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>. Changing a user's password or removing the
      user revokes all of her tokens.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every action a user takes is a row of <code>orca.audit</code>
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/ui#login">The UI</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="worker-tokens">Worker tokens</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`orca worker-token create --pool bgent --label bgent   # prints the token, once
orca worker-token list
orca worker-token revoke 3`} />
  <p>
    A worker token is scoped to one pool. It can claim that pool's runs and make the
    state calls of the runs it claimed, and nothing else; a user's token gets nothing
    from the worker API. There is no read-only or read-write variant. A revoked token
    stays listed as revoked, and the worker using it is refused and exits. Each claim is
    audited as <code>worker:&lt;label&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A run on a worker gets the worker's token as <code>ORCA_STATE_TOKEN</code>, with
    <code>ORCA_STATE_URL</code>, in place of a database. A worker with
    <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> set refuses to start, and it needs only outbound HTTPS
    (<code>--remote</code> takes <code>http</code> only to its own machine), so Postgres
    stays reachable only on the server
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/workers#setup">Remote workers</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="boot-tokens">Boot tokens of autoscaled machines</h2>
  <p>
    A machine of a <a href="/s/orca/docs/pools#autoscaling">pool Orca grows</a> gets a
    worker token of its own, made for it and revoked when it is removed. Its startup
    script holds that token and no other secret. With it, the machine asks the UI's
    <code>/api/worker/boot</code> over TLS for the image, the registry login to pull it,
    and the bucket's variables. <code>/api/worker/boot</code> answers only a token the
    autoscaler made for a machine that is live: one from <code>orca worker-token
    create</code> gets a 403.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bucket's variables come from the UI's own environment, so on Hetzner a pool
    machine gets the same S3 keys as the server. On EC2, give the instances a profile
    whose role reaches the bucket (<code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_PROFILE</code>) instead. The
    machine keeps them in a file only root can read, and deletes the registry login's
    file as soon as it has read it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cluster-token">Cluster tokens</h2>
  <p>
    From 0.24.0, a run spread over several machines of a pool
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/pools#one-run-many-machines">workers = N</a>) gets a token for
    each start. The worker that starts the run makes it and gives it, as
    <code>DISTA_TOKEN</code>, to the run's dista head, its driver and its own dista
    worker, and reports it to the UI. A member gets it only in the answer to its claim,
    over the worker API's TLS. It is never in a log or a page.
  </p>
  <p>
    dista checks it on every connection to a head or a worker's object server: a missing
    or wrong token closes the connection unanswered, and the comparison takes the same
    time wherever the bytes differ. So nothing else on the network can join the run,
    submit to it or fetch its results. It authenticates and doesn't encrypt, so keep the
    pool's machines on a private network, as an autoscaled Hetzner pool does
    (<a href="https://github.com/magmalake/dista.mojo/blob/main/DESIGN.md" rel="noopener">dista's
    DESIGN.md</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="pipeline-secrets">Pipeline secrets</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.gharchive]
secrets = { GITHUB_TOKEN = "orca/github-token" }`} />
  <p>
    A pipeline's <code>secrets</code> map an environment variable to a secret's ID, which
    starts with <code>orca/</code>. Postgres and the run's spec hold only the IDs, and a
    run's page shows its secrets by name only. orca.toml refuses an <code>env</code> name
    that looks like a secret (<code>*TOKEN*</code>, <code>*SECRET*</code>,
    <code>*PASSWORD*</code>, <code>*DATABASE_URL*</code>), so values don't end up there.
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>On EC2</strong>, each instance reads them from Secrets Manager at start with
      its role, which may read only <code>orca/</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>With <code>ORCA_SECRET_FETCH</code></strong>, the scheduler runs that
      command with the ID and uses what it prints. On the Hetzner server it is
      <code>sh /etc/orca/secret-fetch</code>, which prints
      <code>/etc/orca/secrets/&lt;id&gt;</code> (mode 600, root only) and must refuse
      <code>..</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On a remote worker</strong>, the worker's own
      <code>ORCA_SECRET_FETCH</code> reads them there. The spec it claims names the
      secrets and never carries their values.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each run gets <code>ORCA_SECRETS</code>, the names of its secrets, and Orca masks
    their values in every log it writes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deploy-secrets">Secrets on deploy</h2>
  <p>
    On the Hetzner stack, <code>hetzner.env</code> holds no secret itself: it names the
    secret files. A file named <code>*.sops</code> or <code>*.sops.&lt;ext&gt;</code> is
    decrypted with <code>sops -d</code> on its way to the server and never written to a
    file on the laptop, so the laptop keeps one age key instead of plain tokens.
    <code>SECRETS_FILE</code> names one SOPS file with every secret, decrypted once per
    deploy, so a Secure Enclave key asks for Touch ID once. The server gets plain files
    under <code>/etc/orca</code>, mode 600. Every deploy brings them in line and prints
    the names of what changed, never a value
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/deploy#settings">Settings and secrets</a>,
    <a href={`${DOCS}/hetzner.md#secrets-on-your-laptop-sops`} rel="noopener">hetzner.md</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="cloud-credentials">Cloud credentials</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Credential</th><th>Who uses it</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Hetzner API token (read-write)</td>
          <td>the scheduler, to make and remove pool machines</td>
          <td><code>ORCA_HCLOUD_TOKEN</code>, or the secret <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_TOKEN_SECRET</code> names; on the stack, <code>HCLOUD_POOL_TOKEN_FILE</code> sends it to <code>/etc/orca/secrets/orca/hcloud-token</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>AWS credentials</td>
          <td>the scheduler, for EC2 pools and the EC2 runner</td>
          <td><code>AWS_REGION</code> and credentials in its environment, or an instance role</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Instance profile</td>
          <td>EC2 pool machines and run instances, to reach the bucket</td>
          <td><code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_PROFILE</code>, else <code>ORCA_EC2_PROFILE</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Registry login</td>
          <td>the scheduler (to read the image's architectures) and booting machines (to pull it)</td>
          <td><code>ORCA_REGISTRY_USER</code> and the secret <code>ORCA_REGISTRY_TOKEN_SECRET</code> names (<code>orca/ghcr-token</code> on the stack)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>S3 keys</td>
          <td>the server, workers, and autoscaled Hetzner machines</td>
          <td>the stack's <code>s3.env</code>; a worker's <code>worker.env</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ntfy topic and token</td>
          <td>the scheduler, for alerts</td>
          <td><code>ORCA_NOTIFY_SECRET</code>, <code>ORCA_NOTIFY_TOKEN_SECRET</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Hetzner token is sent only as a bearer token and never put in an error, and the
    registry login is never in one either. The firewall each Hetzner pool machine gets
    lets in ssh from <code>ADMIN_IP</code> only
    (<a href={`${DOCS}/hetzner.md#worker-pools-that-grow-themselves`} rel="noopener">hetzner.md</a>).
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>
